this morning there are celebrations in  the streets of Harare the capital of  Zimbabwe residents are hoping this  week's house arrest by the country's  military of longtime President Robert  Mugabe will lead to his ouster and end  decades of political repression and  economic failure Debora patta is  following develops developments from  Johannesburg South Africa Debora good  morning good morning the military  takeover of Zimbabwe has plunged the  country into unchartered territory while  the Army has seized power President  Robert Mugabe is refusing to accede to  their demands to step down tens of  thousands of Zimbabweans took to the  streets calling for Mugabe's resignation  for the first time in nearly four  decades they're hopeful they are  witnessing the dying days of his power  but Mugabe who is world Zimbabwe by fear  for 37 years is not going easily he may  be under house arrest but he's been seen  in public twice since tanks rolled into  the capital of Harare here he was  photographed meeting Zimbabwean generals  and regional officials for talks to  negotiate the terms of his removal from  office 93 years old and in failing  health he is regarded by his critics as  a brutal tyrant who ran the country into  the ground but it was his insistence on  pushing his controversial wife Grace  Mugabe as his successor that was  ultimately his undoing the military and  the ruling Zanu PF party however appear  determined to force Mugabe out by the  end of this weekend exactly how that  will happen is unclear  Anthony Debora patta in Johannesburg  thanks
 

